    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25334_p6_b38",
      "text": "3.3\nTwo-Stage Training Strategy\n",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        237.69,
        252.82,
        247.66
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        432,
        950,
        1011,
        990
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 31,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25334_p6_b39",
      "text": "We adopt a two-stage training strategy to progressively equip GAMSI with spatial understanding\ncapabilities. Stage 1 instills foundational 3D structural and metric-depth perception, while Stage\n2 generalizes these priors to diverse downstream spatial intelligence tasks. Both stages share an\nidentical optimization objective, which we formalize at the end of this subsection for clarity.\n",
      "bbox": [
        107.53,
        258.54,
        504.0,
        301.3
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        430,
        1034,
        2015,
        1205
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 387,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25334_p6_b40",
      "text": "Stage 1: Spatial Perception Alignment. In the first stage, we train GAMSI on the open-source\nSenseNova-SI-800K dataset [22] to instill foundational 3D structural and metric-depth perception\ncapabilities. Through the visual alignment supervision imposed by the EVG module, this stage\nestablishes the geometric priors that are essential for subsequent task-conditioned intelligence.\n",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        307.56,
        504.0,
        350.42
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        432,
        1230,
        2015,
        1401
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 380,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25334_p6_b41",
      "text": "Stage 2: Multi-task Spatial Understanding Fine-tuning. Building upon the foundational 3D\nstructural and metric-depth perception acquired in Stage 1, the second stage aims to generalize\nGAMSI\u2019s capabilities across a broad spectrum of downstream spatial intelligence tasks. To this end,\nwe construct a large-scale multi-task instruction-tuning corpus, termed the MTS (Multi-Task Spatial)\nDataset, by consolidating and re-organizing samples from six publicly available spatial intelligence\ndatasets: CA-VQA [20], MindCube [23], SPAR-7M [24], SpatialMQA [25], SpatialLadder-26k [3],\nand VSI-590K [26]. As summarized in Figure 4, the MTS Dataset contains 152,776 instruction-\nfollowing samples covering 13 spatial task types, and spans three visual modalities in comparable\nproportions: single image (42%), multiple images (32%), and video (26%). To further structure the\ntask space, we organize the 13 task types into two complementary groups: (i) Metric Perception\n(absolute/relative distance, absolute depth, object- and room-level absolute size, relative size); (ii)\nStructural Spatial Perception (relative direction, object count, appearance order, object grounding,\nview change inference, camera motion inference, spatial imagination). Together, these two groups\ncover the spectrum from low-level geometric measurement to high-level structural and viewpoint-\naware perception. The MTS Dataset will be publicly released to facilitate future research on unified\nspatial intelligence.\n",
      "bbox": [
